"      <a> Back to dashboard
      <button> Print / Save as PDF
          <h1> COMMUNITY PLAN
          <p> Generated by EventAI · Delhi
          <span> community
          <span> Date
          <span> 15 Jun 2099
          <span> Location
          <span> Delhi
          <span> Guests
          <span> 300
          <span> Total Budget
          <span> ₹2,00,000
      <p> A neighbourhood Diwali evening for about 300 residents in Pune on a ₹50,000 budget (roughly ₹167 a head), centred on a lane-by-lane rangoli contest, a community faral potluck and a short cultural programme on a small stage. Because the budget is tight we assumed a society courtyard or open ground with no venue fee, a resident-run potluck covering most of the food, and eco-friendly fireworks only if the society and local police allow it. With about six weeks to go, permissions and sponsorship should start immediately.
      <h2> 1. Budget Allocation
          <caption> Budget allocation by category
              <th> Category
              <th> Cost
              <th> %
              <th> Guidance & Tips
              <td> Lighting & Decor
              <td> ₹11,000
              <td> 22 %
              <td> Use string lights in warm white, marigold toran and hand-made diyas from a local potter. Ask residents to lend lanterns and large rangoli stencils.
              <td> Food & Snacks
              <td> ₹13,000
              <td> 26 %
              <td> Run a faral potluck where each household brings one traditional item such as chakli, karanji or ladoo. Pay only for chai, water and a large pot of khichdi or misal for volunteers.
