      <p> A two-day Maharashtrian wedding for 200 guests in Mumbai on a ₹10,00,000 budget: Day 1 brings Halad Chadavne (haldi), mehendi and a family Sangeet, and Day 2 is the Simant Pujan, Antarpat, Mangalashtak and Saptapadi followed by a short evening reception. Catering and the venue take over half the budget, so we assumed a banquet hall in Dadar, Thane or Navi Mumbai rather than a hotel ballroom, a pure-vegetarian traditional menu, and that most guests live in or near Mumbai. With about four months to go, venue, caterer and photographer should be locked in this month because good Muhurat dates book out fast.
      <h2> 1. Budget Allocation
          <caption> Budget allocation by category
              <th> Category
              <th> Cost
              <th> %
              <th> Guidance & Tips
              <td> Venue & Decor
              <td> ₹2,20,000
              <td> 22 %
              <td> Book a banquet hall for two days on a weekday-evening or package rate and ask for the basic stage and lighting to be included. Reuse the Day 2 mandap flowers for the reception backdrop.
              <td> Catering
              <td> ₹3,00,000
              <td> 30 %
              <td> Choose a traditional Maharashtrian thali (puran poli, masale bhaat, varan-bhaat, shrikhand) at ₹900 to ₹1,100 per plate instead of a multi-cuisine buffet, and confirm a final headcount two weeks out to avoid paying for extra plates.
              <td> Photography & Videography
              <td> ₹80,000
              <td> 8 %
              <td> Hire one lead photographer plus one candid shooter for Day 2 and a single photographer for the Day 1 functions. Skip the drone and the printed album, and take a digital gallery instead.
              <td> Attire, Jewellery & Beauty
              <td> ₹1,30,000
              <td> 13 %
              <td> Buy the bride's Paithani or Nauvari saree and rent the groom's pheta and sherwani set. Rent statement jewellery such as the nath and tanmani for the ceremony and use family heirlooms for the rest.
              <td> Priest, Rituals & Music
              <td> ₹50,000
              <td> 5 %
              <td> Ask the priest for one consolidated samagri list and buy it in bulk from a Dadar market wholesaler. Book a single sanai and dhol-tasha group for both the varat and the ceremony.
              <td> Mehendi, Sangeet & Haldi
              <td> ₹60,000
              <td> 6 %
